1. RENDER WITH THE 7386 FIXTURE — PASS
   Ran the repo's own test, unmodified:
   `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... -run 'LivingExpenses|SnapTrap' -v`
   → all PASS, including
   `TestPortfolioSettingsRender_LivingExpensesPhaseNote/aria-valuetext_carries_the_exact_saved_value,_not_the_step-snapped_one`,
   which renders "whatif-portfolio-settings" with MonthlyLivingExpenses=7386
   and asserts `aria-valuetext="$7,386"` is present and `aria-valuetext="$7,400"`
   is absent. Confirmed independently: added a throwaway test in the copy
   (deleted after, never touched the real tree) rendering
   "whatif-quick-adjust-portfolio-content" (the quick-adjust mirror slider's
   defining block) with the same 7386 fixture:
     `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... -run TestVerifyMirrorAriaValueText_7386 -v`
     → PASS: `aria-valuetext="$7,386"` present, "$7,400" absent.
   So both sliders carry the exact value at first paint, matching the display
   span (`${{formatNumber .Settings.MonthlyLivingExpenses}}` — same helper,
   same "$7,386" string) — the pre-interaction gap called out in attempt 1
   ("the snap already applies pre-interaction") is closed on both controls.

2. JS TRACE — every value-changing path updates aria-valuetext — PASS
   - Primary drag/keyboard: range `oninput="onMonthlyLivingExpensesInput(this.value)"`
     (portfolio-settings.html) sets the hidden canonical field's `.value` to
     the raw (browser-snapped, e.g. 7400 while mid-drag — acceptable, see
     below) value and dispatches a bubbling `input` event on it.
   - Document-level capture listener (quick-adjust-scripts.html:207,
     `addEventListener('input', ..., true)`) sees the hidden field's
     `data-quick-adjust-key` (not a mirror) → `syncQuickAdjustKey(key)`.
   - `syncQuickAdjustKey('monthly_living_expenses')` (lines ~101-116) computes
     `exactValueText = '$' + Math.round(Number(canonicalControl.value)||0).toLocaleString()`
     from the hidden field's own value and calls
     `primaryRange.setAttribute('aria-valuetext', exactValueText)` AND
     iterates `getQuickAdjustMirrorControls(key)` setting the same attribute
     on the mirror. Both controls always get the identical string in one
     synchronous call — cannot drift from each other.
   - Mirror drag/keyboard: mirror `input`/`change` → captured, mirror has
     `data-quick-adjust-mirror` → `proxyQuickAdjustMirrorToCanonical` copies
     mirror's value onto the canonical hidden field, dispatches on it, then
     calls `syncQuickAdjustKey` — same update path, same result.
   - Grepped for every other place `.value =` is assigned on either range or
     the hidden field in these three files (`syncQuickAdjustMirrorControls`,
     `proxyQuickAdjustMirrorToCanonical`, `onMonthlyLivingExpensesInput`) —
     all funnel through `syncQuickAdjustKey`'s branch for this key, either
     directly or via the capture listener. No path was found that mutates
     the submitted/displayed value without also running this branch.
   - Confirmed live: `go build ./...` succeeds and
     `go test ./internal/handlers/whatif/... ./internal/templates/...`
     passes in full (22.1s / 0.8s, no failures), so nothing here is only
     true "on paper."

3. aria-valuetext CONTENT FORMAT — PASS
   Server: `aria-valuetext="${{formatNumber .Settings.MonthlyLivingExpenses}}"`
   → "$7,386". Client: `'$' + Math.round(...).toLocaleString()` → "$7,386"
   (verified the two format functions agree: `node -e "console.log('$'+
   Math.round(7386).toLocaleString())"` → "$7,386", matching Go's
   `formatNumber`'s comma-grouped, no-decimal output). It reads as a bare
   value, not a sentence, on both the primary range and the mirror.

